1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,403
Section one's disqualification checklist asked whether AI belongs in this feature at

2
00:00:05,403 --> 00:00:05,644
all.

3
00:00:05,644 --> 00:00:07,268
Assume it passed that test.

4
00:00:07,268 --> 00:00:12,261
This lecture asks the narrower, later question: given that AI helps here, should it

5
00:00:12,261 --> 00:00:16,653
act autonomously, or should a human stay in the loop pulling the trigger.

6
00:00:16,653 --> 00:00:21,646
Four signals answer that question, and you've already met every one of them earlier

7
00:00:21,646 --> 00:00:22,608
in this section.

8
00:00:22,608 --> 00:00:25,075
This lecture just puts them in one place.

9
00:00:25,075 --> 00:00:27,120
That's deliberate, not a shortcut.

10
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:32,113
A checklist built from ideas you've already tested against real examples is one you

11
00:00:32,113 --> 00:00:36,866
can actually defend in a review, because you can point to exactly which earlier

12
00:00:36,866 --> 00:00:41,859
lecture backs each row, instead of asserting caution and hoping it sounds credible.

13
00:00:42,409 --> 00:00:47,725
Read down that left column and notice each phrase is a callback, not a new idea.

14
00:00:47,725 --> 00:00:49,188
Row one is 6.1's test.

15
00:00:49,188 --> 00:00:53,773
Row two is 6.6's blast radius framework finding nowhere safe to land.

16
00:00:53,773 --> 00:00:58,027
Row three is 6.3's table, applied honestly instead of hopefully.

17
00:00:58,027 --> 00:01:03,211
Row four is 6.4's escalation pattern, checked for whether it actually works in

18
00:01:03,211 --> 00:01:03,809
practice.

19
00:01:03,809 --> 00:01:07,065
Do you need to fail every row for this to matter?

20
00:01:07,065 --> 00:01:09,990
No, any single row is sufficient on its own.

21
00:01:09,990 --> 00:01:15,506
A task can pass three of these and fail the fourth, and the fourth is still the one

22
00:01:15,506 --> 00:01:16,370
that matters.

23
00:01:16,370 --> 00:01:18,364
This isn't a score to average.

24
00:01:18,364 --> 00:01:21,221
It's a list of independent reasons to stop.

25
00:01:21,221 --> 00:01:26,139
Treating it as a score is the most common way this checklist gets misused.

26
00:01:26,139 --> 00:01:31,656
"Three out of four, that's mostly fine" is exactly the reasoning that ships the one

27
00:01:31,656 --> 00:01:33,649
row that was never negotiable.

28
00:01:33,649 --> 00:01:39,099
A task that fails row two, no coverable blast radius, doesn't get rescued by acing

29
00:01:39,099 --> 00:01:40,162
the other three.

30
00:01:40,162 --> 00:01:43,751
It gets rescued by fixing row two, or by not shipping.

31
00:01:44,301 --> 00:01:49,137
Same word, refund automation, and the checklist gives two completely different

32
00:01:49,137 --> 00:01:54,158
verdicts because the actual answers are different, not because one request sounds

33
00:01:54,158 --> 00:01:55,956
more cautious than the other.

34
00:01:55,956 --> 00:01:59,365
Notice the good column doesn't pass every row for free.

35
00:01:59,365 --> 00:02:04,449
It passes because someone did the work of bounding the amount, the account status,

36
00:02:04,449 --> 00:02:09,656
and the daily count until the blast radius was actually small and the chain actually

37
00:02:09,656 --> 00:02:10,028
short.

38
00:02:10,028 --> 00:02:12,941
The checklist doesn't reward caution as a vibe.

39
00:02:12,941 --> 00:02:15,793
It rewards a request specific enough to check.

40
00:02:15,793 --> 00:02:20,876
That specificity is also what makes row one, the workflow test, worth re-asking on

41
00:02:20,876 --> 00:02:22,116
the bounded version.

42
00:02:22,116 --> 00:02:27,138
A refund under fifty dollars to a verified account capped at three a day is close

43
00:02:27,138 --> 00:02:32,283
enough to fully specifiable that a plain workflow might handle most of it, with the

44
00:02:32,283 --> 00:02:37,490
agent's judgement reserved for the genuine edge cases a workflow's fixed rules can't

45
00:02:37,490 --> 00:02:38,172
anticipate.

46
00:02:38,172 --> 00:02:43,193
Bounding a proposal often reveals how much of it was never autonomy-shaped in the

47
00:02:43,193 --> 00:02:43,937
first place.

48
00:02:44,487 --> 00:02:49,749
Say the honest caveat, because a section this focused on what goes wrong can leave

49
00:02:49,749 --> 00:02:55,076
the impression that agents rarely clear the bar, and that's not the honest finding.

50
00:02:55,076 --> 00:03:00,402
Most proposals that fail this checklist fail on an unbounded version of a perfectly

51
00:03:00,402 --> 00:03:04,316
reasonable idea: no cap, no gate, no defined escalation path.

52
00:03:04,316 --> 00:03:09,578
Bound it the way the good column just did, and the same idea often passes cleanly.

53
00:03:09,578 --> 00:03:14,968
The checklist's real job is forcing that bounding to happen before launch, not after

54
00:03:14,968 --> 00:03:17,535
the version with no cap already shipped.

55
00:03:17,535 --> 00:03:22,861
Genuine disqualifications, the idea itself being wrong rather than under-specified,

56
00:03:22,861 --> 00:03:26,648
are real but rarer than they feel from inside this section.

57
00:03:26,648 --> 00:03:31,653
A task with catastrophic, irreversible downside and no way to add a checkpoint

58
00:03:31,653 --> 00:03:36,466
anywhere in its chain is a real row-two failure no amount of scoping fixes.

59
00:03:36,466 --> 00:03:39,610
Most requests that land on your desk aren't that.

60
00:03:39,610 --> 00:03:44,102
They're an unbounded good idea waiting for someone to do the bounding.

61
00:03:44,652 --> 00:03:49,586
That's the checklist worth keeping, and running it with real numbers is the entire

62
00:03:49,586 --> 00:03:52,895
difference between this exercise and a vague gut check.

63
00:03:52,895 --> 00:03:57,949
Most people can guess which row their proposal is weakest on before they even finish

64
00:03:57,949 --> 00:03:58,491
counting.

65
00:03:58,491 --> 00:04:03,424
Counting anyway is what turns that guess into something you can actually defend in

66
00:04:03,424 --> 00:04:06,312
the review where someone asks you to justify it.

67
00:04:06,312 --> 00:04:10,163
Write the failing row down specifically, not as a general worry.

68
00:04:10,163 --> 00:04:15,097
"Row two, no way to cap or gate the refund amount" is something an engineer can go

69
00:04:15,097 --> 00:04:15,939
fix this week.

70
00:04:15,939 --> 00:04:20,753
"I have a bad feeling about this one" is not, and it's the version most unscoped

71
00:04:20,753 --> 00:04:24,603
concerns arrive in if nobody forces the specific question first.

72
00:04:25,153 --> 00:04:26,835
One thing before you move on.

73
00:04:26,835 --> 00:04:30,664
Run the full checklist, not just the row that already worried you.

74
00:04:30,664 --> 00:04:34,086
The row you weren't watching is usually the one that fails.

75
00:04:34,086 --> 00:04:38,901
Then ask the harder follow-up: a failure from unbounded scope is fixable today, but

76
00:04:38,901 --> 00:04:43,715
a failure baked into the fundamental idea, something no amount of bounding rescues,

77
00:04:43,715 --> 00:04:48,414
is worth saying so plainly, and saying it now costs far less than saying it after

78
00:04:48,414 --> 00:04:48,820
launch.

79
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:53,460
Before the workshop next lecture, where you'll scope one of these for real, make

80
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:56,360
sure you can tell the two failures apart on sight.

81
00:04:56,360 --> 00:05:00,305
That distinction is most of what this lecture was actually teaching.
